// Cs2.grammar — C# 2.0: generics (type-level) (T3.1.1.1).
// Generic type names (C<T>, C<T,U>, qualified N.List<T>), type-parameter lists on type
// declarations (class/struct/interface/delegate), and variance (in/out).
//
// T0.3 merge semantics: re-declaring a rule name APPENDS alternatives (declaration order across
// files); it does NOT modify the Cs1 alternative. The engine is longest-match-wins and an
// equal-length tie resolves to the FIRST alternative (Cs1, loaded first). So a re-declared Cs2
// alternative is reachable only when it is MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with the Cs1 one.
//
// REQUIRED-new-construct principle: the Cs2 re-declared alternative REQUIRES the new construct,
// so it cannot match the same input at the same length as the Cs1 alternative.
//   TypeName: Cs1 = !ReservedKeyword Identifier (non-generic); Cs2 = !ReservedKeyword Identifier
//   TypeArgumentList (generic, REQUIRED). `C` -> Cs1 (Cs2 fails: no "<"); `C<T>` -> Cs2 (longer).
//   No tie.
//
// Boundary decision D1 (declarations): the merged TypeName is longest-match, so in a declaration
// context it GREEDILY consumes `C<T>` as a generic type name. Consequences:
//   * a non-variance generic declaration (`class C<T> { }`) is matched by the Cs1 ClassDeclaration
//     (greedy TypeName + ClassBody); the Cs2 ClassDeclaration (TypeName + REQUIRED TypeParameterList)
//     then sees `{` where it needs `<` and fails. Only one alternative matches -> no tie.
//   * a variance declaration (`interface I<in T>`, `delegate void D<out T>(T x);`) is matched by the
//     Cs2 alternative: the greedy TypeName cannot consume `<in T>` (in/out are reserved -> not a Type
//     -> not a TypeArgumentList), so TypeName stops at the name and the REQUIRED TypeParameterList
//     matches `<in T>`. The Cs1 alternative fails (body/base-list sees `<`). Only one matches -> no tie.
// In every case the Cs1 and Cs2 alternatives are mutually exclusive (never an equal-length tie):
// whenever the greedy TypeName leaves a `<...>` for the TypeParameterList, that `<...>` is not a
// TypeArgumentList, so the Cs1 body/base-list cannot follow it. Full hand-traces in this file's
// progress doc (docs/CSharpParserPlan-progressT3.1.1.1.md).
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Parser/LanguageParser.cs):
//   ParseTypeParameterList (6104): "<" (TypeParameter,)+ ">" (requireOneElement, allowTrailingSeparator:false).
//   ParseTypeParameter (6155): [attributes] (in|out)? identifier.
//   ParseTypeArgumentList (6545): "<" (Type,)+ ">".
//   Declaration position — class/struct/interface (1824) and delegate (5844): name, then
//   TypeParameterList, then (delegate: parameter list / class etc.: base list), then body.
//   Variance in/out are reserved keywords (already in Cs1 ReservedKeyword), so neither can be a
//   TypeName and they disambiguate cleanly in TypeParameter = VarianceModifier? TypeName.
//
// CS2-only: no default type parameters `= ...` (CS4), no method type parameters / constraints
// (T3.1.1.2). Variance on non-interface/delegate is a binder concern (parser accepts, binder rejects).

// === Generic type name (T3.1.1.1). Re-declares Cs1 TypeName; the Cs2 alternative REQUIRES the
// TypeArgumentList, so it is mutually exclusive with the Cs1 non-generic alternative (no tie). ===
TypeName = !ReservedKeyword Identifier TypeArgumentList;

// Type argument list: "<" (Type,)+ ">". Arguments are full Types, so C<int[]> and C<Foo<T>> work.
// Roslyn ParseTypeArgumentList (LanguageParser.cs:6545).
TypeArgumentList = "<" (Type; ",")+ ">";

// === Type parameter list on type declarations (T3.1.1.1). Roslyn ParseTypeParameterList (6104). ===
TypeParameterList = "<" (TypeParameter; ",")+ ">";

// Type parameter: [variance] name. Roslyn ParseTypeParameter (6155): (in|out)? identifier.
TypeParameter = VarianceModifier? TypeName;

// Variance modifier: in | out. Both are reserved keywords (Cs1 ReservedKeyword), so neither can be
// a TypeName; the `?` keeps the modifier unambiguous in TypeParameter (a bare in/out cannot be
// consumed by TypeName). Named union (the meta-grammar does not allow `|` inside a group).
VarianceModifier =
    | "in"
    | "out";

// === Re-declared type declarations (T3.1.1.1): TypeParameterList REQUIRED. ===
// The Cs2 alternative REQUIRES the TypeParameterList in the Roslyn position (after the name, before
// the base list / parameter list). Reachable for the variance case; for the non-variance case the
// Cs1 alternative (greedy TypeName) wins (Boundary decision D1 above). Mutually exclusive either way.
ClassDeclaration = Attributes? ClassModifier* "class" TypeName TypeParameterList BaseList? ClassBody ";"?;

StructDeclaration = Attributes? StructModifier* "struct" TypeName TypeParameterList BaseList? StructBody ";"?;

InterfaceDeclaration = Attributes? InterfaceModifier* "interface" TypeName TypeParameterList BaseList? InterfaceBody ";"?;

DelegateDeclaration = Attributes? DelegateModifier* "delegate" Type TypeName TypeParameterList "(" ParameterList? ")" ";";

// ============================================================================
// T3.1.1.2 — C# 2.0 method type parameters + constraint clauses.
//
// Method type parameters (`void M<T>(T x) { }`): the `<T>` is consumed by the GREEDY merged
// TypeName (Boundary decision D1, T3.1.1.1), so no explicit TypeParameterList is needed in the
// method header. At v1 (non-greedy TypeName) `void M<T>()` REJECTS (TypeName stops at `M`, then
// `(` is expected but `<` is found); at v2 the greedy TypeName eats `M<T>` and the Cs1 Method
// alternative matches. So method type parameters are parse-reachable at v2 via the Cs1 Method
// alternative; the REQUIRED-new-construct that makes the Cs2 Method alternative reachable is the
// ConstraintClause+ below.
//
// Constraint clauses (Roslyn ParseTypeParameterConstraintClauses:2242 /
// ParseTypeParameterConstraintClause:2250 / ParseTypeParameterConstraint:2346, LanguageParser.cs).
// CS2-only: a constraint is one of `struct` | `class` | `new()` | a Type (an interface / base
// class / another type-parameter name — the parser parses a Type, the binder decides what it is).
// No `unmanaged` (CS8), `notnull` (CS8), `enum<T>` (CS7.3), `delegate` (CS7.3), `default` (CS9),
// or `allows ref` (CS13).
//
// `where` is NOT a Cs1 ReservedKeyword (checked: absent from the list). It is matched here as a
// WordLiteral (raw whole-word text match), so no ambiguity arises: a constraint clause is only
// recognized where the rule explicitly expects `"where"`, and the clause requires `where <id> :`
// (Roslyn IsCurrentTokenWhereOfConstraintClause:2234), so a bare `where` cannot be mistaken for a
// type name / base list.
//
// The re-declared Method / InterfaceMethod / type declarations REQUIRE ConstraintClause+ (>= 1
// clause) as the REQUIRED-new-construct, so each is mutually exclusive with the Cs1 and T3.1.1.1
// alternatives (never an equal-length tie). Full hand-traces in
// docs/CSharpParserPlan-progressT3.1.1.2.md.

// Constraint clause: `where` + type-parameter-name + `:` + (constraint,)+. Roslyn
// ParseTypeParameterConstraintClause (LanguageParser.cs:2250). The type-parameter-name is a simple
// true identifier (Roslyn IsTrueIdentifier / ParseIdentifierName), hence `!ReservedKeyword Identifier`.
ConstraintClause = "where" !ReservedKeyword Identifier ":" ConstraintList;

// Comma-separated constraint list, one or more, no trailing comma (SeparatedList Forbidden).
// Roslyn: first bound + `,` loop (LanguageParser.cs:2261-2309).
ConstraintList = (Constraint; ",")+;

// A single constraint (CS2): struct | class | new() | Type. Named union (the meta-grammar forbids
// `|` inside a group). `struct`/`class`/`new` are reserved keywords (Cs1 ReservedKeyword), so none
// can be a Type; the four alternatives are mutually exclusive. Roslyn ParseTypeParameterConstraint
// (LanguageParser.cs:2346), CS2 subset.
Constraint =
    | "struct"
    | "class"
    | NewConstraint = "new" "(" ")"
    | Type;

// === Re-declared methods (T3.1.1.2): ConstraintClause+ REQUIRED. ===
// The greedy TypeName consumes the method's `<...>` type-parameter list, so the disambiguating
// REQUIRED-new-construct is the ConstraintClause+ (Roslyn position: after the parameter list,
// before the body — ParseMethodDeclaration, LanguageParser.cs:3691-3698). Cs1 Method (no
// constraints) and the T3.1.1.2 Method (constraints) are mutually exclusive: a method with no
// constraint clause is matched by Cs1 (MethodBody right after `)`), and a method with a constraint
// clause is matched by T3.1.1.2 (Cs1 MethodBody = Block | ";" cannot start with `where`).
Method = Attributes? MethodModifier* Type TypeName "(" ParameterList? ")" ConstraintClause+ MethodBody;

// Interface methods end with `;` (no body); constraints appear before the `;`.
InterfaceMethod = Attributes? MethodModifier* Type TypeName "(" ParameterList? ")" ConstraintClause+ ";";

// === Re-declared type declarations (T3.1.1.2): ConstraintClause+ REQUIRED. ===
// As in T3.1.1.1 (D1), the greedy TypeName consumes the `<...>` type-parameter list, so the
// T3.1.1.2 alternative does NOT repeat the TypeParameterList; it places ConstraintClause+ after the
// base list (Roslyn position: name, type-params, base list, constraints, body —
// ParseMainTypeDeclaration, LanguageParser.cs:1839-1843). The T3.1.1.1 alternative (with
// TypeParameterList) remains reachable for the variance case; the T3.1.1.2 alternative
// (ConstraintClause+) is reachable for the non-variance-with-constraints case. Mutually exclusive
// (no tie): a base-list/body position is either `{`/`:`/`<` (Cs1 / T3.1.1.1) or `where` (T3.1.1.2).
ClassDeclaration = Attributes? ClassModifier* "class" TypeName BaseList? ConstraintClause+ ClassBody ";"?;

StructDeclaration = Attributes? StructModifier* "struct" TypeName BaseList? ConstraintClause+ StructBody ";"?;

InterfaceDeclaration = Attributes? InterfaceModifier* "interface" TypeName BaseList? ConstraintClause+ InterfaceBody ";"?;

DelegateDeclaration = Attributes? DelegateModifier* "delegate" Type TypeName "(" ParameterList? ")" ConstraintClause+ ";";

// ============================================================================
// T3.1.2 — C# 2.0 `var` (implicit typing) + anonymous methods.
//
// `var` is a PLAIN IDENTIFIER in C# 1.0 but a RESERVED KEYWORD in C# 2.0. It is therefore NOT
// added to Cs1 ReservedKeyword (that would break C# 1.0, where `var` is a valid type/variable
// name). Instead it is APPENDED to the merged ReservedKeyword here (T0.3 merge semantics), so the
// merged ReservedKeyword reserves `var` only at version 2+.
//   * v1 (Cs1 only): `var` is not reserved -> a valid TypeName (`!ReservedKeyword Identifier`) ->
//     `var x = 5;` parses as a *typed* declaration whose type is `var`.
//   * v2 (Cs1+Cs2): `var` is reserved -> not a TypeName -> not a Type -> the Cs1
//     LocalVariableDeclaration (Type-based) cannot start with `var`; the Cs2 `"var"` alternative
//     (REQUIRED-new-construct) matches instead. Mutually exclusive, no tie.
//
// Anonymous methods are EXPRESSIONS (Roslyn ParseAnonymousMethodExpression, LanguageParser.cs:13734):
//   `delegate` + optional `(params)` + `{ block }`. They are added to Primary (append, the same
//   pattern Cs11 uses for raw strings). Disambiguation with DelegateDeclaration is by CONTEXT: an
//   anonymous method is a Primary (parsed only in expression position), a delegate declaration is a
//   TypeDeclaration (parsed only in namespace/class-member position). After `delegate`, a type
//   identifier leads to a declaration; `{` or `(` leads to an anonymous method. The two never
//   compete at the same position (no equal-length tie).
//
// CS2-only: no `async`/`partial`/`static` anonymous-function modifiers (CS5/CS2-modifier/CS8), no
// default parameter values (`delegate (int x = 0)`, a binder concern handled by Parameter — out of
// scope here), no lambdas (CS3, T3.2).
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Parser/LanguageParser.cs):
//   ParseAnonymousMethodExpression (13734) -> worker (13743): eat DelegateKeyword, optional
//   ParseParenthesizedParameterList (4750) when next is "(", then required ParseBlock.
//   ParseParameter (4938): modifiers + ParseType + identifier (the untyped `x` form is a
//   binder/version concern — implicit-typed anonymous-method parameters are C# 4.0; the parser
//   accepts the syntactic form, parser-vs-binder split).
//   Syntax tests: ExpressionParsingTests.cs:1953 (typed) / :1984 (empty list) / :2014 (no list);
//   DeclarationParsingTests.cs:12669-12670 (var + anonymous method), :12787 (ref param).

// === `var` reservation (version-purity). Re-declares Cs1 ReservedKeyword; APPENDS "var". ===
ReservedKeyword =
    | "var";

// === `var` local variable declaration (C# 2.0 implicit typing). Re-declares Cs1
// LocalVariableDeclaration; the Cs2 alternative REQUIRES the "var" literal (REQUIRED-new-
// construct), mutually exclusive with the Cs1 Type-based alternative (at v2 var is not a Type).
// Roslyn ParseLocalDeclarationStatement (LanguageParser.cs:10482). `var x;` (no initializer)
// parses at the syntax level — a binder error CS0815 in real C#. ===
LocalVariableDeclaration = "var" VariableDeclarator ("," VariableDeclarator)* ";";

// === Anonymous method (C# 2.0 expression). Re-declares Primary (append, as Cs11 does for raw
// strings). `delegate` + optional parameter list + block. Roslyn ParseAnonymousMethodExpression. ===
Primary =
    | AnonymousMethod = "delegate" AnonymousMethodParameterList? Block;

// Anonymous method parameter list: "(" (param,)* ")" — empty parens allowed (`delegate () { }`).
// Roslyn ParseParenthesizedParameterList (LanguageParser.cs:4750), requireOneElement:false.
AnonymousMethodParameterList = "(" (AnonymousMethodParameter; ",")* ")";

// Anonymous method parameter: explicitly typed (`int x`) OR untyped name (`x`). Named union (the
// meta-grammar forbids `|` inside a group). Longest-match: `int x` -> typed (untyped fails: int is
// reserved, not a TypeName); `x` -> untyped (typed fails: needs a 2nd name); `Foo x` -> typed
// (longer than the untyped `Foo`).
AnonymousMethodParameter =
    | AnonymousMethodTypedParameter = ParameterModifier* Type TypeName
    | AnonymousMethodUntypedParameter = TypeName;

// ============================================================================
// T3.1.3 — C# 2.0 `partial` modifier (class/struct/method) + `sealed override`.
//
// `partial` is a CONTEXTUAL KEYWORD in Roslyn (an IdentifierToken whose ContextualKind is
// PartialKeyword; LanguageParser.cs:1324-1328 maps it to DeclarationModifiers.Partial in the generic
// ParseModifiers loop). It is therefore NOT added to ReservedKeyword (T1.3.1 decision: modern Roslyn
// treats it as contextual, mcs ISO_1 demotes it to an identifier, and it is merge-friendly —
// append-only merging cannot remove an alternative from ReservedKeyword). Instead it is APPENDED to
// the relevant Cs1 modifier unions (T0.3 merge semantics), so the merged modifiers accept `partial`
// only at version 2+.
//
// Version-purity: `partial` is a plain identifier in C# 1.0 (not reserved, not a modifier), so at v1
// `partial class C { }` / `partial struct S { }` / `partial void M();` REJECT (partial is not a
// modifier and cannot start a declaration); at v2 the merged modifiers accept `partial` and they
// PARSE. No Cs1 modification.
//
// Partial methods: a *declaration* `partial void M(int x);` (void, NO body, ends with `;`) and an
// *implementation* `partial void M(int x) { }` (WITH a body). The Cs1 Method (Cs1:265) already allows
// both: its MethodBody (Cs1:267) is the named union `| Block | ";"`. So once `partial` is a
// MethodModifier BOTH forms parse via the Cs1 Method alternative — the `;` and `{` bodies
// disambiguate inside MethodBody (different leading tokens, no tie). No re-declaration of Method is
// needed. The CS2 void-only return-type restriction is a BINDER concern (Roslyn CS0759/CS8023), not a
// parse concern: the parser accepts `partial int M();` syntactically (parser-vs-binder split).
//
// `sealed override`: `sealed` is a HARD keyword (SyntaxKindFacts.cs:974-975) and is ALREADY a Cs1
// MethodModifier (Cs1:299). There is NO CSharpVersion gate for sealing an override method (a C# 1.0
// feature), so `sealed override void M() { }` already parses at v1 (and v2) via the Cs1 Method
// alternative — no Cs2 change required.
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Parser/LanguageParser.cs):
//   ParseModifiers (1347) -> GetModifierExcludingScoped (1286-1344): PartialKeyword -> Partial
//   (1318-1319 hard; 1324-1328 contextual). IsCurrentTokenDefinitelyPartialModifier (1637) gates
//   `partial` on a following type/member-declaration start.
//   Syntax tests: RoundTrippingTests.cs:1579 PartialMethodWithLanguageVersion2
//   (`partial class P { partial void M(); }` parses cleanly at CSharp2);
//   ParserErrorMessageTests.cs:5502 PartialMethodsVersionThree (`partial int Goo() { }` — non-void;
//   the void-only restriction is a binder CS8023 at CSharp2, a parse-level success).

// === `partial` modifier (T3.1.3): re-declare Cs1 modifier unions, APPEND "partial". ===
// T0.3 merge: re-declaring a rule name APPENDS alternatives. The Cs1 ClassDeclaration/StructDeclaration/
// Method use these merged modifier unions, so no re-declaration of the declarations is needed.
// `"partial"` is a WordLiteral (RuleGenerator.cs:77-79) and matches whole-word (partial, not partialX).
ClassModifier =
    | "partial";

StructModifier =
    | "partial";

MethodModifier =
    | "partial";
